{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"SCOPE OF THE OVERRIDE (recording here so it is on the ticket, not just in chat): `[tool.uv] override-dependencies` is a WORKSPACE RESOLUTION DIRECTIVE. It is consumed by uv when resolving/locking THIS repo and is NOT published in nava-rebar wheel metadata. Consequences:\n\n* A consumer running `pip install nava-rebar[eval]` from PyPI is UNAFFECTED - they still get inspect-ai s own `click!=8.2.0,<8.2.2,>=8.1.3` cap and resolve click 8.2.1. Behaviour unchanged for them; we have not pushed a click floor onto downstream users.\n* The override moves ONLY this repo s committed uv.lock and the envs synced from it (CI, contributor worktrees).\n\nSo the blast radius is this repo s own environments, which is exactly where the gating pip-audit job runs.\n\nCI COVERAGE OF inspect-ai UNDER THE NEW LOCK - nothing in CI exercises it, and it is STRUCTURALLY unable to:\n1. inspect-ai is only in the `[eval]` extra. The pip-audit job installs `uv sync --locked --extra dev` (no eval), so inspect-ai is not installed there; click 8.4.2 reaches the audited set via uvicorn and other dev deps, not via inspect-ai.\n2. pyproject `[tool.uv] conflicts` declares `[{extra=\"eval\"}, {extra=\"dev\"}]` mutually conflicting (\"nothing installs dev+eval together\"). The pytest suite runs off the dev extra, so inspect-ai CANNOT be present in the tested set - this is not \"we happened not to test it\".\n3. rebar imports inspect_ai lazily behind `guard_import(..., extra=\"eval\")`, so those paths abstain when it is absent.\n4. 1463 selected tests pass under the new lock, 0 failures.\n\nHONEST LIMIT ON THE STALENESS EVIDENCE: I verified inspect-ai 0.3.251 imports under click 8.4.2 and that `inspect --help` / `inspect eval --help` render and exit 0 - that exercises click s Command/Option construction and the group->subcommand dispatch chain, which is where an 8.2.2+ API break (BaseCommand/MultiCommand removal, parser/context changes) would raise. I did NOT run a live eval task end-to-end (needs model credentials). Tracked as a follow-up ticket, discovered_from this one; the gap PREDATES this change.\n\nON THE zipfile-zstd MARKER in the lock diff: not caused by the override. inspect-ai requires zipfile-zstd only under `python_full_version < 3.14`, and zipfile-zstd unconditionally requires `zstandard>=0.15.0`; uv propagates the parent marker down to that transitive edge. Both uv 0.7.18 and 0.12.1 emit it, so the committed lock was simply stale on it and ANY relock corrects it. It narrows zstandard s marker to exactly the range where its only requirer is installed - no change to the installed set on any Python CI tests (3.11/3.12/3.13)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785713145652992001,"uuid":"9ba87fb6-fcb5-43f7-982b-7b28aeb5bb96"}